# Prompt Rules

1.  Scope
    A.  These rules apply to how I respond to prompts.
    B.  They do not replace any other spec/rule files.

2.  Apply rule files to paul files
    A.  When editing any file in `paul/`, apply the
        formatting + layout rules in `.cursor/rules/outline.mdc`.

3.  Chat log
    A.  For every prompt, append a new entry to
        `paul/chat_log.txt`. Do this unless the user
        explicitly asks you not to.
    B.  Never skip chat log updates. Treat it as mandatory
        for every response that addresses a prompt.
    C.  Entry header format: `N.  LABEL – YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS AM`.
    D.  LABEL is a 1–3 word summary in ALL CAPS.
    E.  Entry body format:
        1.  A.  Prompt
            a.  Quote the prompt verbatim.
        2.  B.  Response
            a.  1–3 concise bullets of what changed/done.
        3.  C.  Codenames
            a.  List codenames involved in the prompt effort.
            b.  Mark new ones (e.g. prefix with "new:" or
                append "(new)").
            c.  If none, write "None."
            d.  Role executed every prompt: update
                paul/codename_index.txt when codenames are
                added, removed, or changed (codename.mdc
                §3B3).
    F.  For very small changes, keep Response to 1 bullet.
    G.  The entry must follow the outline format rules in
        `.cursor/rules/outline.mdc` (markers, alignment, wrap).
    H.  Prompt classification: determine whether the prompt
        is about manipulating/creating the queued `##  NEXT
        PROMPT` entry or about doing other work. Either way,
        update `paul/chat_log.txt` (and apply the queue rules
        in section 4 when relevant).

4.  Processed vs queued prompts
    A.  When a prompt asks for next-prompt suggestions or
        to populate/update the queued prompt, update the
        `##  NEXT PROMPT` entry in place with the new
        options. Also add a chat log entry for that prompt.
    B.  `paul/chat_log.txt` contains one separator line:
        `------------------`
    C.  Entries above the separator are PROCESSED prompts.
    D.  The separator must appear immediately after the most
        recent processed entry.
    E.  Entries below the separator are QUEUED prompts.
    F.  QUEUED prompt entries:
        1.  Header: `##  LABEL` (ALL CAPS; no number, no
            date/time).
        2.  Body: three options (A/B/C).
            a.  `A.  <SHORT NAME>`
                1.  <prompt bullet 1>
                2.  <prompt bullet 2, if needed>
                3.  ...
            b.  `B.  <SHORT NAME>`
                1.  <prompt bullet 1>
                2.  <prompt bullet 2, if needed>
                3.  ...
            c.  `C.  <SHORT NAME>`
                1.  <prompt bullet 1>
                2.  <prompt bullet 2, if needed>
                3.  ...
        3.  No Response section until processed.
        4.  Useful choices (apply when generating or updating
            `##  NEXT PROMPT`):
            a.  Base options on context: recent work, spec
                gaps, obvious next steps, or user-curated
                ideas.
            b.  Each option must be concrete and actionable;
                no vague placeholders (e.g. "Continue" or
                "Proceed as needed").
            c.  Option labels (SHORT NAME) in A, B, C: 2–3
                words; same brevity rules as the chat log
                entry LABEL that becomes the final title.
            d.  Mix types: e.g. one polish, one new feature,
                one refactor.
        5.  Avoid generic fallbacks when context suggests
            specific alternatives.
    G.  When a queued prompt is executed:
        1.  Move it above the separator.
        2.  Add date/time and a Response section.
        3.  Keep exactly one queued prompt ahead (one entry
            below the separator).
        4.  When a queued prompt is executed, the processed
            entry's A.  Prompt section must contain the FULL
            prompt: the selected option's complete text
            (all bullets), verbatim — not a placeholder like
            "Do next prompt C". Add final item: "Executed
            next prompt."
    H.  If a prompt changes the queued prompt (but does not
        execute it), update the queued `##  NEXT PROMPT`
        entry in place. Do not add a second queued prompt.
    I.  Update `##  NEXT PROMPT` after EVERY processed prompt.
        This is mandatory. Re-examine the options and refresh
        or revise as warranted; it may remain unchanged.
        1.  If recent prompts show the user is carefully
            curating the queued prompt, be conservative:
            prefer minimal edits (grammar/clarity) over
            changing intent.
        2.  Still correct obvious grammar or formatting
            problems when you see them.
    J.  If a prompt is focused on modifying `##  NEXT PROMPT`,
        do not execute the queued prompt. Only update the
        queued `##  NEXT PROMPT` entry in place.
    K.  If `##  NEXT PROMPT` contains A/B/C options:
        1.  User selects by writing something like:
            a.  "Execute next prompt: A"
            b.  "Execute next prompt: B"
            c.  "Execute next prompt: C"
        2.  Abbreviations are allowed (case-insensitive):
            a.  "Do next a"
            b.  "Do next b"
            c.  "Do next c"

5.  Resetting the chat log
    A.  If a prompt asks to delete/reset the chat log, replace
        `paul/chat_log.txt` with a fresh log starting at entry
        `1.`.
    B.  Preserve the separator + exactly one queued `##  NEXT
        PROMPT` entry below it.

6.  Spec maintenance (primary app features)
    A.  If a prompt creates or modifies a feature in the
        primary application (outside `paul/`), update the
        relevant spec file(s) in `paul/` (spec_*.txt)
        accordingly.
    B.  Use the outline format rules in `.cursor/rules/outline.mdc`.
    C.  Add or modify the spec in the appropriate location,
        with careful, intelligent organization (do not just
        append at the end).
    D.  Minimal as necessary: keep spec edits as small as
        possible while still correct and complete (avoid
        unrelated reflows/rewording).
    E.  Re-examine the spec on every processed prompt. Only
        change it when the prompt actually touched a primary
        app feature.
